# Primary-source grounding and teaching boundaries

The miniature reconstructs arguments, not a linear sequence of settled discoveries. The three desks are a focused prototype of a different play grammar, not comprehensive intellectual history. Texts below were consulted directly; optional in-game quotations are short and attributed separately from invented cases.

## Socrates in Plato

[Plato, Apology 22d–e, Jowett translation](https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/apology.html) distinguishes genuine craft competence from an unjustified claim to wisdom in other important matters. The sky forecaster is invented. The Babylonian watch supplies an actual earlier-created achievement to which that distinction can be applied; no direct Babylon-to-Socrates transmission is asserted.

[Plato, Republic I, Jowett translation](https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/republic.2.i.html) supplies the weapon-return problem (331c–d), mistaken-ruler objection and expert-ruler rejoinder (339b–341a), and the physician/shepherd dispute (342–343). The miniature paraphrases the repayment aspect rather than pretending to reproduce the entire first definition of justice. Grain return and the physician’s smaller fee are authored particular cases. The threatening weapon demand adapts Plato’s example rather than quoting it. The two commitments—obeying rulers and serving their advantage—are both explicit before the mistaken-order counterexample. The expert reply remains arguable, not certified as true by a successful combination. A shepherd’s care can serve an owner: the result does not claim that every caring act is exploitation. The miniature does not reproduce all Socrates’ subsequent replies.

## Plato’s positive proposal and Cynic challenge

[Republic VI, especially484–489 and505–506](https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/republic.7.vi.html) connects philosophical knowledge, the good and rule. The positive case is placed beside the shepherd’s rival account, not synthesized into a compulsory answer. The navigator certificate is a modern application of the competence-domain problem, not a recorded institutional proposal.

[Diogenes Laertius, Lives VI.38, Hicks translation](https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diogenes_Laertius/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/6/Diogenes*.html) transmits the Alexander/sun anecdote. The text is much later than Diogenes; the game marks late testimony and does not claim eyewitness certainty. Targeted reopens of the Chicago page intermittently timed out; the VI.38 locator and narrative were also checked against the [translated primary-text excerpt](https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/diogenes-laertius-the-cynics-and-stoics.pdf). The explicit patron-necessity claim is an authored prompt. Its result establishes only that this particular need is met without becoming the ruler’s dependent—not that society, care, institutions or other people’s needs disappear.

## Marcus: correction, not indifference to others

[Marcus Aurelius, Meditations IV.3–4 and12, Long translation](https://classics.mit.edu/Antoninus/meditations.4.four.html) provides judgment practice, common rational community, the providence-or-atoms alternative, and willingness to revise a judgment for justice or common benefit. The hungry citizen, insult and blanket imperial return order are modern teaching cases, not reported historical acts. Stoic claims about judgment are historical positions being examined, not present-day medical advice or a denial of external injury. The earlier Cynic case is deliberately bounded by another person’s need; the earlier repayment objection materially revises the fictional order. Direct dependence of Marcus on these exact cases is not alleged.

## Chronology and retained astronomy

The original Babylon opening, its sources and its earned watch calendar are copied exactly from source51. No Copernican, Keplerian or Galilean observation is sent backward into Greek philosophy. The full original astronomical discovery campaign remains accessible in the preserved snapshot. This miniature only tests a philosophical branch after the ancient opening; it is not a claim to have retested or improved the original later astronomy.

## Deliberate limits

Historical utterances are not automatically true because they make a game recipe. Reconstructed rivals and objections are not an exhaustive account of these traditions. Crossing an earlier result into a later invented case is a pedagogical comparison unless the source note explicitly establishes transmission. Further traditions remain deferred until the bounded case grammar earns positive play evidence; no coverage or recipe quota justifies padding.
